17:["$","main",null,{"id":"top","children":[["$","header",null,{"className":"border-b border-ink/10 bg-surface/40","children":["$","div",null,{"className":"mx-auto max-w-5xl px-5 py-10 sm:px-8","children":[["$","$Ld",null,{"href":"/operating-system","className":"inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 text-sm font-semibold text-mute transition hover:text-accent","children":"← All chapters"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"mt-5 flex items-center gap-2 text-sm font-semibold text-accent","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"rounded-full bg-accent/12 px-2.5 py-0.5","children":"Chapter 1"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"text-mute","children":["· ",16," min read · illustrated"]}]]}],["$","h1",null,{"className":"mt-3 font-hand text-5xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-6xl","children":"What an Operating System Actually Is"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-lg text-mute","children":"Two views of one program: the friendly face on top, the ruthless referee underneath"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"mt-6 flex max-w-2xl flex-col gap-3","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/85","children":"Ask ten engineers what an operating system is and you will get ten fuzzy answers: \"it is Windows\", \"it is the thing that boots\", \"it is the kernel\". None of these are wrong, but none of them are the whole picture either. The honest answer is that an OS is best understood from two directions at once, and holding both in your head is the single most useful mental model in this entire course."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/85","children":"Looked at from above, from where your code sits, the OS is an abstraction layer. It takes the ugly, model-specific reality of hardware and dresses it up as a small set of clean, portable ideas: files, processes, memory. You call open(\"data.txt\") and never once think about which disk controller is attached or how its firmware wants to be poked."}],["$","p","2",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/85","children":"Looked at from below, from where the hardware sits, the OS is a resource manager. One CPU, a few gigabytes of RAM, one disk, and dozens of programs that were never written to cooperate, all demanding those resources at the same time. The OS decides who gets what, when, and for how long, and enforces the rules so nobody cheats. This chapter makes that duality concrete and builds the vocabulary the rest of Part A depends on."}]]}]]}]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"mx-auto flex max-w-6xl gap-10 px-5 py-12 sm:px-8","children":[["$","nav",null,{"className":"sticky top-8 hidden h-fit w-56 shrink-0 lg:block","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"mb-3 font-hand text-xl font-bold text-ink","children":"On this page"}],["$","ol",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-1.5 text-sm","children":[["$","li","01-two-views",{"children":["$","a",null,{"href":"#01-two-views","className":"flex gap-2 rounded-md px-2 py-1 text-mute transition hover:bg-accent/10 hover:text-accent","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"text-accent/70","children":"01"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"leading-snug","children":"Two views of the same program"}]]}]}],["$","li","02-abstraction",{"children":["$","a",null,{"href":"#02-abstraction","className":"flex gap-2 rounded-md px-2 py-1 text-mute transition hover:bg-accent/10 hover:text-accent","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"text-accent/70","children":"02"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"leading-snug","children":"Abstraction: turning ugly hardware into clean ideas"}]]}]}],["$","li","03-resource-management",{"children":["$","a",null,{"href":"#03-resource-management","className":"flex gap-2 rounded-md px-2 py-1 text-mute transition hover:bg-accent/10 hover:text-accent","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"text-accent/70","children":"03"}],"$L21"]}]}],"$L22","$L23","$L24"]}]]}],"$L25"]}],"$L26"]}]
